      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To investigate interleukin-2-induced antinociception in morphine-insensitive rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Paw withdrawal latencies (PWL) to noxious radiant heat were taken as the measurement of pain threshold."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Intraplantar injection of human recombinant interleukin-2 (1.5 x10(4) U) significantly increased PWL in normal rats. PWL was also markedly increased by IL-2 in 45-d-post-complete Freund's adjuvant (CFA)-treated rats, which have been proven morphine-insensitive. IL-2-induced antinociception in CFA-treated rats were significantly lower than that in normal rats. IL-2-induced antinociception was partially blocked by naloxone (1 mg/kg, ip) in normal rats but remained unchanged in CFA group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "IL-2-induced antinociception is partially mediated by mu-opioid receptors. Therapeutic applications of IL-2 may also be expanded to relieve morphine-insensitive pain."
        }
      ]},
